  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe rest of today through Monday...\n\nA shortwave in the flow moves through this evening, bringing a \nchance (20-50%) for showers and thunderstorms through Wednesday\nmorning. No severe storms are expected, but any thunderstorm will\nbe capable of producing wind gusts around 40 mph and pockets of \nheavy rain.\n\nThe rest of Wednesday into the end of the work week, temperatures\nwill trend warmer under an upper level ridge. Highs increase from\n70s Wednesday to upper 70s to low 80s for Thursday and Friday. \nConditions will be mostly dry, although models are in better \nagreement for a weak shortwave to slide through Wyoming and \neastern Montana Friday, bringing a low chance (10-30%) for showers\nand thunderstorms east of Billings.\n\nLate Friday into Saturday, a trough moves northeast from western\nMontana into Canada. An associated mostly dry cold front will \nallow highs to be much cooler Saturday, in the 60s to low 70s. \nSince the trough passes north of the region, precipitation is \nunlikely, with generally a less than 20% chance. However, the cold\nfront will allow for widespread breezy conditions of 20 to 35 mph\nFriday afternoon into Saturday, with some stronger gusts in the \nmountains. With the warmer temperatures Friday, there are some \nfire weather concerns, although these concerns are limited by the \nrecent widespread rain.\n\nSunday into early next week, the upper level pattern looks to\nremain progressive, allowing a 20-50% chance for showers and\nthunderstorms each day and temperatures to decrease to near to\nslightly below normal, in the 60s, by Monday. Archer",
